<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Imperial City of Dortmund</h1><p class="st-meta">1220 CE – 1803 CE (approx.) · capital Dortmund</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Westphalian imperial city on the Hellweg, a leading Hanseatic town that held off Cologne in the Great Feud of 1388 to 1389.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Dortmund grew around a royal court on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellweg">Hellweg</a>, the trade road between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a>. Ottonian kings held assemblies there, and by around 1220 it was the only imperial city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>. Its merchants traded across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> and Baltic, and it became a leading member of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A council of merchant patricians ruled the city. Its free court was the leading tribunal of the Vehmic courts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-dortmund-feud">Great Dortmund Feud</a> of 1388 to 1389 it withstood a siege by the Archbishop of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-cologne">Cologne</a> and the Count of the Mark. The city was surrounded by Mark territory and gradually lost trade. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> was introduced in the 1560s and 1570s.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1803 Dortmund lost its freedom and was given to the house of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-nassau-diez">Nassau-Orange</a>. It belonged to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grand-duchy-of-berg">Grand Duchy of Berg</a> from 1808 and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> from 1815, growing into a great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="imperial city, 1220 CE – 1803 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">imperial city</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:83.98%;width:11.60%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1220 CE – 1803 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>imperial city</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dortmund</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Westphalian Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dortmund</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1512&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-dortmund" aria-label="Free Imperial City of Dortmund on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4849 395 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free Imperial City of Dortmund lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5169 651L5229 694M5169 651L5183 683"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5183 683h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5169 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5169 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Dortmund. <a href="/atlas?year=1512&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-dortmund">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
